<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Poland (1916-1918)</h1><p class="st-meta">1916 CE – 1918 CE · capital Warsaw</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom without a king, created by Germany and Austria-Hungary in occupied Poland during the First World War, which handed power to Pilsudski in 1918.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After driving the Russians out of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/congress-poland">Congress Poland</a> in 1915, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> proclaimed an independent Kingdom of Poland by the Act of 5 November 1916, hoping to recruit Polish soldiers. Its borders and future king were left undecided.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A Provisional Council of State, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski">Jozef Pilsudski</a>, was set up in 1917, but real power remained with the German governor-general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. When Pilsudski's legionnaires refused an oath of loyalty to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>, he was interned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regency-council-kingdom-of-poland">Regency Council</a> of Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleksander-kakowski">Aleksander Kakowski</a>, Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zdzislaw-lubomirski">Zdzislaw Lubomirski</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-ostrowski">Jozef Ostrowski</a> then governed, building Polish courts, schools and offices.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> collapsed in November 1918, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regency-council-kingdom-of-poland">Regency Council</a> handed command of the army and then all authority to Pilsudski, freshly released, and dissolved itself. Its institutions passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-poland-1916-1918.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Poland (1916-1918)" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Alex Tora or Alex K in Ukranian and Japanese wiki · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Poland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1916 CE – 1918 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Warsaw</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aleksander Kakowski, Zdzislaw Lubomirski, Jozef Ostrowski</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Poland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Regency Kingdom of Poland</dd></dl></aside></div>
